    "display_name": "Knitted Scarf",
    "long_instructions": "You’re building a multiple linear regression model that predicts how long it will take to knit a scarf (in hours) based on two variables: the number of rows of stitches and the number of stitch types in the scarf’s design. You have the following training data:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n| Rows | Stitch Types | Time |\r\n| - | - | - |\r\n| 100 rows | 8 stitch types | 6 hours |\r\n| 180 rows | 2 stitch types | 5 hours |\r\n| 250 rows | 6 stitch types | 9 hours |\r\n| 320 rows | 1 stitch types | 8 hours |\r\n| 400 rows | 4 stitch types | 12 hours |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. The first training data example has already been added to the `inputs` list. Add the remaining training data examples to the `inputs` list.\r\n2. The first training data example has already been added to the `outputs` list. Add the remaining training data examples to the `outputs` list.\r\n3. Train the model based on the training data inputs and outputs.\r\n4. Run the code and make some predictions. Check that they seem reasonable based on your training data.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nTo train a linear regression model with `scikit-learn`, recall that you can use code like:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nmodel.fit(x, y)\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhere `x` represents the inputs and `y` represents the outputs.\r\n",
